omite, which share the structure, grow as octahedra whose edges and corners may be truncated by d{110} and a{100}.","row_title":"Dodecahedron {110}","col_title":"Cube {100}","rows":[{"label":"d0","detail":"absent"},{"label":"d1","detail":"minor"},{"label":"d2","detail":"moderate"},{"label":"d3","detail":"strong"}],"cols":[{"label":"a0","detail":"absent"},{"label":"a1","detail":"minor"},{"label":"a2","detail":"moderate"}],"note":"Chromite (a ≈ 8.34 Å) has the same Fd3̄m structure and the same convex habits. Spinel-law twins on {111} and the skeletal, hopper and dendritic crystals of rapidly grown chromian spinel are not modelled."},"about":"<p>Chromian spinel is a key petrogenetic indicator: its Cr# records the degree of mantle melting and the tectonic\nsetting (Dick &amp; Bullen 1984; Barnes &amp; Roeder 2001). In MORB, rapid diffusion-controlled growth produces\nskeletal and hopper crystals whose Cr# zoning follows their morphology (Roeder et al. 2001). Magnetite, on its own page,\nis the Fe³⁺ member of the same spinel structure.</p>","habits":[{"name":"Octahedron","text":"Row d0, column a0."},{"name":"Edge-bevelled","text":"d{110} truncations (rows d1–d3)."}],"references":[{"citation":"Finger, L.W., Hazen, R.M., Hofmeister, A.M. (1986). High-pressure crystal chemistry of spinel (MgAl<sub>2</sub>O<sub>4</sub>) and magnetite (Fe<sub>3</sub>O<sub>4</sub>): comparisons with silicate spinels. <i>Physics and Chemistry of Minerals</i> 13, 215–220.","doi":"10.1007/BF00308271","url":null,"note":"Structure.","tags":["lattice"]},{"citation":"Bosi, F., Biagioni, C., Pasero, M. (2019). Nomenclature and classification of the spinel supergroup. <i>European Journal of Mineralogy</i> 31, 183–192.","doi":"10.1127/ejm/2019/0031-2788","url":null,"note":"Current spinel-supergroup nomenclature.","tags":["review"]},{"citation":"Dick, H.J.B., Bullen, T. (1984). Chromian spinel as a petrogenetic indicator in abyssal and alpine-type peridotites and spatially associated lavas. <i>Contributions to Mineralogy and Petrology</i> 86, 54–76.","doi":"10.1007/BF00373711","url":null,"note":"Cr# as an indicator of melting and tectonic setting.","tags":["petrology"]},{"citation":"Barnes, S.J., Roeder, P.L. (2001). The range of spinel compositions in terrestrial mafic and ultramafic rocks. <i>Journal of Petrology</i> 42, 2279–2302.","doi":"10.1093/petrology/42.12.2279","url":null,"note":"Compositional fields of chromian spinel by rock type.","tags":["petrology"]},{"citation":"Roeder, P.L., Poustovetov, A., Oskarsson, N. (2001). Growth forms and composition of chromian spinel in MORB magma: diffusion-controlled crystallization of chromian spinel. <i>The Canadian Mineralogist</i> 39, 397–416.","doi":"10.2113/gscanmin.39.2.397","url":null,"note":"Skeletal and hopper chromian spinel; morphology tied to Cr# zoning.","tags":["morphology"]}],"cells":[{"id":"d0a0","row":0,"col":0,"label":"d0a0","sub":"","title":"d0a0: dodecahedron absent · cube absent","description":"dodecahedron absent; cube absent.","default":true,"params":{"face_distances":{"111":1.0,"110":null,"100":null},"cap_translation":0.0,"scale":0.52455753,"planes":{"111":0.52455753}},"fractions":{"111":1.0},"metrics":{"volume":1.0,"surface_area":5.71911,"rsv":0.52456,"sphericity":0.84558,"aspect":1.6882},"geometry":{"vertices":[[-0.90856,-0.0,0.0],[0.0,-0.90856,0.0],[-0.0,0.0,-0.90856],[0.0,0.0,0.90856],[0.90856,0.0,-0.0],[0.0,0.90856,0.0]],"faces":[{"form":"111","hkl":[1,1,1],"loop":[5,3,4]},{"form":"111","hkl":[-1,1,1],"loop":[5,0,3]},{"form":"111","hkl":[-1,-1,-1],"loop":[1,0,2]},{"form":"111","hkl":[-1,-1,1],"loop":[3,0,1]},{"form":"111","hkl":[1,-1,1],"loop":[3,1,4]},{"form":"111","hkl":[1,-1,-1],"loop":[4,1,2]},{"form":"111","hkl":[1,1,-1],"loop":[4,2,5]},{"form":"111","hkl":[-1,1,-1],"loop":[2,0,5]}]},"files":{"stl":"models/stl/spinel/spinel_d0a0.stl","step":"models/step/spinel/spinel_d0a0.step"}},{"id":"d0a1","row":0,"col":1,"label":"d0a1","sub":"","title":"d0a1: dodecahedron absent · cube minor","desc